<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Susquehannock</h1><p class="st-meta">1550 CE – 1675 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Iroquoian people of the lower Susquehanna valley, powerful middlemen in the 17th-century fur trade until wars with the Iroquois and colonists broke them.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Susquehannock moved down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehanna-river">Susquehanna River</a> into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> in the 16th century, settling near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancaster-pennsylvania">Lancaster</a> in large palisaded towns. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-smith-explorer">John Smith</a> met them in 1608 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake Bay</a> he described them as tall and well armed. They spoke an Iroquoian language but were long-standing enemies of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Controlling routes between the interior and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware</a> bays, the Susquehannock traded beaver pelts for European goods with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-netherland">New Netherland</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-sweden">New Sweden</a>, which supplied them with guns. Their towns were run by councils of chiefs, and they farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and hunted. In 1652 they made a treaty with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-maryland">Province of Maryland</a>, ceding land along the bay.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Epidemics and wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seneca-nation">Seneca</a> wore them down, and in 1675 they abandoned their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a>. Attacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> militia at their fort on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potomac-river">Potomac</a>, their raids helped spark <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacons-rebellion">Bacon's Rebellion</a>. Survivors joined the Iroquois; the last Conestoga were killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paxton-boys">Paxton Boys</a> in 1763.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/susquehannock.jpg" alt="Susquehannock" width="320" height="200"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Susquehannock_lang.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1550 CE – 1675 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iroquoian traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Susquehannock</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United States</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Conestoga, Andaste</dd></dl></aside></div>

